{"step_index":212,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-24T17:40:57Z","input_tokens":2481,"cache_read_tokens":121512,"output_tokens":1962,"thinking":"The goal is to determine the connection between user identifiers (user\\_id, email) and resources within the dataset. Initial inspection focuses on identifying relevant evidence files likely to contain linking information. The search is for files that might map users to resources or provide a data model for resource access tied to user identities.\n\n\nDirectly examining data records, including SQL inserts and JSON data, revealed the primary identifiers used for linking user information to resources are email addresses, user IDs, phone numbers, or email hashes. Initial investigation suggests the data model consistently uses these identifiers as key linking elements. Now, need to see if the JSON files have the same properties to map to this model.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"python3 -c \"\nimport pandas as pd, json, glob, sqlite3\n\nsample_req = pd.read_csv('dataset/sample_deletion_requests.csv')\nsample_out = pd.read_csv('dataset/sample_output.csv')\nusers = pd.read_csv('dataset/user_identities.csv')\naudit = pd.read_csv('dataset/user_audit_log.csv')\nmanifest = pd.read_csv('dataset/evidence_files.csv')\n\n# Build identity graph for each user\nuser_graphs = {}\nfor _, u in users.iterrows():\n    uid = u['user_id']\n    user_graphs[uid] = {\n        'user_id': uid,\n        'emails': {str(u['email']).strip().lower()} if pd.notna(u['email']) else set(),\n        'phones': {str(u['phone']).strip()} if pd.notna(u['phone']) else set(),\n        'hashes': {str(u['email_hash']).strip().lower()} if pd.notna(u['email_hash']) else set(),\n        'names': {str(u['name']).strip().lower()} if pd.notna(u['name']) else set(),\n    }\n\nemail_to_uid = {}\nfor uid, g in user_graphs.items():\n    for em in g['emails']:\n        email_to_uid[em] = uid\n\nfor _, a in audit.iterrows():\n    uid = a['user_id']\n    if uid not in user_graphs:\n        user_graphs[uid] = {'user_id': uid, 'emails': set(), 'phones': set(), 'hashes': set(), 'names': set()}\n    fn = a['field_name']\n    for val in [a['old_value'], a['new_value']]:\n        if pd.notna(val):\n            val_s = str(val).strip()\n            if fn == 'email':\n                user_graphs[uid]['emails'].add(val_s.lower())\n                email_to_uid[val_s.lower()] = uid\n            elif fn == 'phone':\n                user_graphs[uid]['phones'].add(val_s)\n            elif fn == 'name':\n                user_graphs[uid]['names'].add(val_s.lower())\n\nprint(f'Indexed {len(user_graphs)} users and {len(email_to_uid)} emails.')\n\n# Test on case_01 to case_05\nfor idx, r in sample_req.merge(sample_out, on='case_id').head(5).iterrows():\n    cid = r['case_id']\n    req_email = str(r['email']).strip().lower()\n    uid = email_to_uid.get(req_email)\n    g = user_graphs.get(uid)\n    steps = json.loads(r['steps_json'])\n    expected_res = [s['resource_id'] for s in steps]\n    print(f'{cid} ({uid}): Expected resources: {expected_res}, User emails: {g[\\\"emails\\\"]}')\n\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolSummary":"Verify user identity resolution for sample cases"}}]}
